-### Instanced Voxelization
-Uses instancing to draw all slices in a single draw call. The instance ID specifies the target slice, enabling efficient voxelization of deforming meshes or obstacles [S2, unofficial].
+### Voxel Instancing
+A technique to repeat the same object many times in a scene by storing the object's model only once in memory and storing additional information (position and orientation) for each occurrence, reducing memory footprint and increasing rendering speed [S3, unofficial]. The source model for all identical instances is called a 'class'. In voxel form, the class stores voxel values defining the object, which can be stored in a regular grid, an adaptive grid (like an octree), or compressed using Run-Length-Encoding (RLE) [S3, unofficial]. An instance is a small piece of information that records where the instance is located and to which class it belongs [S3, unofficial]. Class size: Compressed voxel classes may take one or two megabytes each [S3, unofficial]. Modifying instanced voxels based on position (e.g., environmental effects like snow or moss) is not practical because it requires processing time and additional storage to remember changes, negating the speed and memory benefits of instancing [S3, unofficial]. Unlike polygonal instancing which operates at the polygon level, voxel instancing operates on voxel data, offering constant memory footprint and fast loading times for the instances [S3, unofficial].
 
-### Sphere Impostors for DEM Pebbles
-Rendering of Discrete Element Method (DEM) pebble beds using instanced sphere impostors. Each pebble is drawn as a quad with an exact orthographic ray-sphere hit test in the fragment shader, providing sphere shading and depth. This allows rendering 27,000 pebbles at interactive rates with only 1.2-1.3 ms of CPU overhead per frame. If no wgpu device is available, egui discs are used as a fallback [S3, unofficial].
+### Splat quad vertex layout
+Sets up a 4-vertex quad with specific coordinates for shader-based scaling of projected 2D Gaussian eigenvectors [S4, unofficial]. Define vertex positions: (-2,-2), (2,-2), (2,2), (-2,2). Define indices: 0,1,2,0,2,3. Create vertex buffer with VET_FLOAT2 at VES_POSITION. Create index buffer with IT_16BIT. Bind to render operation [S4, unofficial]. Vertex buffer: 4 vertices, 2 floats per vertex (VET_FLOAT2, VES_POSITION). Index buffer: 6 indices, 16-bit (IT_16BIT). Instance buffer: 1 float per instance (VET_FLOAT1, VES_TEXTURE_COORDINATES[0], location 8) [S4, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Setup time is $O(V)$ to compute offsets and pack data. Draw time is $O(1)$ draw call, with fragment shader cost proportional to visible voxels [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Setup time is $O(V)$ to compute offsets and pack data, where $V$ is the number of voxels [S1, unofficial]. Draw time is $O(1)$ draw call, with fragment shader cost proportional to visible voxels [S1, unofficial].
 *   **Memory Complexity:** $O(V)$ for the instance buffer (5 floats per voxel) and $O(P)$ for the palette texture [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Implemented in the VoxelRenderer Android application for rendering .vly voxel models using OpenGL ES 3.0 [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Reduces CPU-GPU synchronization overhead by batching all voxels into a single draw call. Simple implementation using standard OpenGL features.
-*   **Limitations:** Requires a palette texture, which may limit color depth or require dithering. Lighting is simple Phong, which may not suffice for complex materials. Visibility culling is not inherent to the instancing mechanism itself and must be handled separately (e.g., frustum culling) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Implemented in the VoxelRenderer Android application for rendering `.vly` voxel models using OpenGL ES 3.0 [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Reduces CPU overhead by minimizing draw calls; efficient memory usage for static voxel data; simple lighting model [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Limited to uniform voxel sizes (unit cubes); lighting is per-voxel, not per-pixel surface accurate; palette texture size limits material variety [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** Static or semi-static voxel scenes with limited material variety and moderate resolution [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When Not to Use:** Scenes requiring dynamic per-voxel modification (e.g., erosion, snow accumulation) as it negates instancing benefits [S3, unofficial]; scenes requiring high-resolution surface detail beyond unit cubes [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-This method is implemented in the VoxelRenderer Android application for rendering .vly voxel models using OpenGL ES 3.0 [S1, unofficial]. It builds on the principles of [Voxels rasterized as geometry](27-voxels-rasterized-as-geometry.md), specifically leveraging the instancing variant to optimize draw call overhead.
+Originated in the context of voxel-based game engines and visualization tools, where instancing was adapted from polygonal graphics to voxel data [S3, unofficial]. The specific implementation using palette textures and per-instance offsets was detailed in the VoxelRenderer project for Android [S1, unofficial]. Voxel instancing concepts were further discussed in procedural world generation contexts, highlighting the benefits of constant memory footprint and fast loading times for compressed voxel classes [S3, unofficial].
 
